## Why This Is Asked
This question tests cross-platform native module design, real-time constraints, memory management, and safe bridging with JavaScript.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time audio pipeline, native threads, DSP paths, and cross-platform bridging
- Lock-free data structures, sample-rate negotiation, backpressure handling, and deterministic scheduling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal API usage sketch
import { startEngine, feedInput, onProcessed, stopEngine } from 'NativeAudio';

startEngine({ sampleRate: 48000, channels: 1 });
feedInput(inputBuffer, length);
onProcessed((processedBuffer) => { /* handle processed audio */ });
stopEngine();
```

## Architecture Considerations
- **Threading**: Run audio processing on a dedicated high-priority thread (real-time priority on Android, a dedicated dispatch queue on iOS) separate from the JS thread to avoid blocking.
- **Memory**: Use lock-free ring buffers and pre-allocated buffer pools to ensure zero allocations on the hot path.
- **Backpressure**: Implement a bounded queue with clear semantics for what happens when the input buffer fills—either drop frames, signal overflow, or block gracefully based on the use case.
- **Sample-rate changes**: Negotiate sample rate at engine start; if the device doesn't support the requested rate, fall back to the nearest supported rate and notify JS.
- **Fallback**: Detect DSP capability at runtime; if unavailable, either gracefully degrade or expose a clear error indicating the device lacks required hardware support.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design offline-first, distributed data synchronization with real-world constraints (latency, privacy, scale) in a mobile fleet context. Emphasizes secure, conflict-aware replication without centralized bottlenecks.

## Key Concepts
- WebRTC DataChannels for direct device sync
- CRDT-based namespaces for idempotent merges
- Vector clocks for causality, tombstones for deletes
- End-to-end encryption and minimal key management
- Garbage collection strategy for tombstones and stale data

## Code Example
```javascript
// useP2PQueue.ts
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { P2PSyncManager } from './syncManager';

export const useP2PQueue = () => {
  const [pendingOps, setPendingOps] = useState([]);
  const [isConnected, setIsConnected] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    const sync = new P2PSyncManager();
    sync.onConnectionChange(setIsConnected);
    sync.onPendingOpsChange(setPendingOps);
    return () => sync.disconnect();
  }, []);

  const enqueue = async (namespace, operation) => {
    await syncManager.enqueueOperation(namespace, operation);
  };

  return { pendingOps, isConnected, enqueue };
};
```

## Trade-offs
- **Latency**: Direct P2P reduces round-trip time vs cloud relay
- **Privacy**: Data stays on-device, no third-party exposure
- **Scalability**: Mesh topology scales linearly but requires connection management

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-platform native module design, covering model loading, thread management, memory control, and privacy. It probes data marshaling between JS and native layers, lifecycle management, and the ability to handle streaming inference workloads.

## Key Concepts

- TurboModule / JSI integration for high-performance native-JS communication
- Cross-platform backend: TF Lite on Android, Core ML on iOS
- Dedicated worker thread with a ring buffer for input queueing
- Memory budgeting and backpressure via bounded queues
- Privacy-conscious data handling: keep inference on-device, avoid unnecessary data copies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Usage sketch
import { RNMLInference } from 'react-native-ml-infer';

async function runInference() {
  // Initialize the model on a dedicated worker thread
  await RNMLInference.startModel('/models/classifier.tflite', [1, 224, 224, 3], [1, 1000]);

  // Stream input frames and receive results via callback
  RNMLInference.onResult((result) => {
    console.log('Inference result:', result);
  });

  // Feed streaming input (e.g., camera frames)
  for (const frame of cameraStream) {
    await RNMLInference.feedInput(frame);
  }

  // Cleanup when done
  await RNMLInference.stopModel();
}
```

## Thread-Safety & Backpressure

- **Thread Safety**: Use atomic operations or lock-free data structures for the input queue; the worker thread consumes inputs while JS pushes them. Ensure all native object access is guarded by mutexes or serialization queues.
- **Backpressure**: Implement a bounded ring buffer. When the queue is full, either drop the oldest entry or signal back to JS to slow production. This prevents unbounded memory growth.
- **Lifecycle Cleanup**: Invalidate the worker thread on stopModel(); use std::promise/std::future or React Native's native module lifecycle callbacks (invalidate()) to ensure all threads terminate and memory is freed before the module is unloaded.

